WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.620
Sanitizers are defensive runtime analysis tools that help C++ teams find serious defects earlier, while the code is still close to the developers who can fix it.

2
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:17.080
They instrument a build so that certain classes of memory misuse or undefined behavior are detected during execution.

3
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:26.540
That makes them especially valuable for codebases where a defect may remain quiet until a rare input, boundary condition, or shutdown path finally exercises it.

4
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:32.580
AddressSanitizer, commonly called ASan, focuses on many memory-access problems.

5
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:40.920
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, or UBSan, focuses on operations where C++ rules no longer define reliable behavior.

6
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:49.340
Both are useful because memory defects and undefined behavior can become reliability, maintainability, and security risks.

7
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:58.720
They can cause crashes, corrupt data, invalidate review assumptions, and make compiler optimizations interact with broken code in surprising ways.

8
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.880
Sanitizers are not proof that software is safe.

9
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:06.860
They generally report problems in code paths that actually run under the instrumented build.

10
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.240
If tests do not exercise an unsafe path, the sanitizer may never observe it.

11
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:16.500
A clean sanitizer run is useful evidence, not a certificate.

12
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:21.500
The strongest teams treat sanitizer builds as one layer in a broader safety workflow.

13
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:34.320
Safer abstractions, ownership clarity, bounds-aware APIs, static analysis, code review, unit tests, integration tests, and targeted modernization all still matter.

14
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:44.020
Sanitizers add a powerful feedback loop: they make certain hidden failures visible enough for engineers to investigate, fix root causes, and prevent regressions.

15
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.660
That feedback loop is most valuable when it is routine.

16
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:58.480
Developers should know how to run a sanitizer build locally, reviewers should understand what a report means, and leaders should treat findings as engineering work rather than optional cleanup.

17
00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:07.300
Used consistently, sanitizers help teams discover risky behavior before it becomes expensive, ambiguous, or buried in a larger release.
